// Domain 1: Design Secure Architectures (30%)
// Schema: id, d (domain), q ("scenario\n\nquestion"), o (options), a (indices of correct options), r (one rationale per option).
// Options are shuffled at runtime, so rationales never refer to option letters.
window.QUESTIONS = (window.QUESTIONS || []).concat([
{ id: "d1-01", d: 1,
  q: "A company uses one AWS account for development and another AWS account for production. Members of the operations team sign in to the development account as IAM users. The team must be able to start, stop, and reboot Amazon EC2 instances in the production account. The company does not want team members to keep a second set of long-term credentials for the production account.\n\nWhat should a solutions architect do to meet these requirements?",
  o: ["In the production account, add an IAM role with EC2 permissions and a trust policy for the development account. Allow the team to assume the role.",
      "In the development account, add an IAM role with EC2 permissions that name the production instance ARNs. Attach the role to the team's IAM group.",
      "Create IAM users for the team in the production account with EC2 permissions. Store each user's access key in AWS Secrets Manager in the development account.",
      "Share the production EC2 instances with the development account by using AWS Resource Access Manager (AWS RAM). Grant the team EC2 permissions in the development account."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A role in the production account holds the EC2 permissions, and its trust policy lets principals from the development account assume it. Team members switch to the role and get temporary credentials, so they keep only their existing sign-in.",
      "A policy in the development account cannot grant access to resources in another account on its own. The production account must trust the caller, which needs a role or a resource policy in the production account.",
      "This creates a second set of long-term credentials for each team member, which the company wants to avoid. Storing the keys in Secrets Manager does not change that they are long-term keys.",
      "AWS RAM shares resources such as subnets and transit gateways. It does not share EC2 instances, so the team would still have no access to the production instances."] },

{ id: "d1-02", d: 1,
  q: "A startup created its AWS account with a shared team email address. The founders use the root user for daily administration, and the root user has an active access key that a deployment script uses. An audit finds that the account does not follow AWS security best practices for the root user.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Delete the root user access key. Give the deployment script an IAM role with only the permissions that the script needs.",
      "Turn on multi-factor authentication (MFA) for the root user. Give each founder individual administrative access through IAM Identity Center.",
      "Rotate the root user access key every 90 days. Store the new access key in AWS Secrets Manager for the deployment script to read.",
      "Turn on MFA for the root user. Keep the root user for daily administration and send an alert to the founders after each root sign-in.",
      "Attach an IAM policy to the root user that denies billing actions. Keep the root user for daily work by the founders."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["AWS recommends that the root user have no access keys. A role with least privilege gives the script temporary credentials instead of the most powerful credentials in the account.",
      "MFA protects the root user, and individual access through IAM Identity Center means nobody needs the root user for daily administration. Together they follow the root user best practices.",
      "Rotating the key still leaves a long-term root access key in use. The best practice is to remove root access keys entirely, not to rotate them.",
      "MFA is a good step, but the root user should be kept for the few tasks that need it. Alerts after each sign-in do not stop the risky daily use.",
      "IAM policies cannot be attached to the root user, which always has full access to the account. The root user would also stay in daily use."] },

{ id: "d1-03", d: 1,
  q: "A company manages 25 member accounts through AWS Organizations. The security team must make sure that no user or role in any member account can stop or delete the AWS CloudTrail trails that the security team created. This restriction must also apply to administrators in the member accounts and to accounts that join the organization later.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Attach a service control policy (SCP) to the organization root. Deny cloudtrail:StopLogging and cloudtrail:DeleteTrail in the SCP.",
      "Create an IAM policy that denies cloudtrail:StopLogging and cloudtrail:DeleteTrail. Attach the policy to every IAM user and role in each member account.",
      "Create an AWS Config rule in each account that detects stopped trails. Add an automatic remediation that turns logging back on for each stopped trail.",
      "Create an Amazon EventBridge rule in each account that matches StopLogging events. Invoke an AWS Lambda function that turns logging back on."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An SCP sets the maximum permissions for every principal in the member accounts, including administrators. Attached to the root, it also covers accounts that join later, with one policy to maintain.",
      "Administrators in each account could detach or edit the policy, and each new user, role, and account needs the policy added. This is more work and does not stop administrators.",
      "This reacts after a trail has already stopped, so some activity goes unrecorded. It also needs a rule and remediation in every account.",
      "This also reacts after the fact, and someone could still delete a trail. It needs a rule and a function in every account."] },

{ id: "d1-04", d: 1,
  q: "A company has 40 AWS accounts in AWS Organizations. Employees already sign in to all business applications through a third-party identity provider (IdP) that supports SAML 2.0 and SCIM. The company wants employees to use their existing credentials to access the AWS accounts. Access must be assigned by job function and must be removed automatically when an employee leaves.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Turn on AWS IAM Identity Center with the IdP as the identity source. Provision users and groups through SCIM and assign permission sets to groups for each account.",
      "Create an IAM SAML identity provider and federated roles in each of the 40 accounts. Map the IdP groups to the roles in each account.",
      "Turn on AWS IAM Identity Center with its built-in identity store. Create a user for each employee and assign permission sets to groups for each account.",
      "Create IAM users for each employee in a central identity account. Let the users assume roles in the other accounts that match their job function."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["IAM Identity Center connects to the IdP once for all accounts in the organization. SCIM keeps users and groups in sync, so removing an employee in the IdP removes access, and permission sets map job functions to accounts.",
      "Federation in each account works, but the company would maintain a SAML provider and roles in 40 accounts. That is far more work than one central connection.",
      "The built-in identity store gives employees a second set of credentials. Someone would also have to remove each user when an employee leaves.",
      "IAM users are separate long-term credentials that employees must manage. They are also not removed when the IdP account is disabled."] },

{ id: "d1-05", d: 1,
  q: "A company is building a mobile app that lets users sign in with their Google or Apple accounts. After sign-in, each user uploads photos directly to an Amazon S3 bucket. Each user must be able to write only to an S3 prefix that matches the user's own ID, and the app must not contain any AWS access keys.\n\nWhat should a solutions architect do to meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Use an Amazon Cognito identity pool to give users temporary AWS credentials. Use an IAM policy with a policy variable for the identity ID in the S3 prefix.",
      "Use an Amazon Cognito user pool for sign-in and create an IAM user for each new user. Return the IAM user's access key to the app after each sign-in.",
      "Use an Amazon Cognito identity pool to give users temporary AWS credentials. Use an IAM policy that grants s3:PutObject on the whole bucket to signed-in users.",
      "Send each photo to an Amazon API Gateway REST API that writes the object to Amazon S3. Use an API key in the app to identify each user."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An identity pool exchanges the Google or Apple token for temporary credentials for an IAM role. The policy variable for the Cognito identity ID limits each user to a prefix that matches that user's ID.",
      "This hands long-term IAM access keys to the app, which the company does not allow, and it would create an IAM user for every app user.",
      "Temporary credentials remove the stored keys, but every signed-in user could write to every other user's prefix.",
      "An API key identifies the app, not the user, and anyone who extracts it can call the API. The photos also no longer go directly to Amazon S3."] },

{ id: "d1-06", d: 1,
  q: "An application reads and writes objects in one Amazon S3 bucket from Amazon EC2 instances that an Auto Scaling group launches. The launch template passes an IAM user's access key and secret key to the instances in user data. A security review requires the company to remove long-term credentials from the instances.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements MOST securely?",
  o: ["Create an IAM role with access to only that bucket. Attach the role to the instances through an instance profile in the launch template.",
      "Store the access key and secret key in AWS Secrets Manager. Grant the instances permission to read the secret when they start up.",
      "Create an IAM role with access to only that bucket. Have the application call AWS STS GetSessionToken with the IAM user's keys at startup.",
      "Encrypt the access key and secret key with an AWS KMS key before they go into user data. Decrypt the keys in the application at startup."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An instance profile delivers the role's temporary credentials through the instance metadata service, and the SDK rotates them automatically. No long-term keys exist on the instances, and the role is limited to the one bucket.",
      "The access key is still a long-term credential, now fetched from a different place. It can still leak from the instance and does not expire.",
      "GetSessionToken still starts from the IAM user's long-term keys, which must stay on the instance. The new role is also never used.",
      "Encryption hides the keys in user data, but the application still holds long-term keys after it decrypts them."] },

{ id: "d1-07", d: 1,
  q: "A company lets its developers create IAM roles for the AWS Lambda functions that they deploy. The security team is concerned that a developer could create a role with more permissions than the developer has, such as full access to IAM. The security team must cap the permissions of any role that developers create, without reviewing each role.\n\nWhat should a solutions architect do to meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create a managed policy that sets the maximum permissions. Let developers create roles only when that policy is attached as the role's permissions boundary.",
      "Create a managed policy that sets the maximum permissions. Set it as the permissions boundary of each developer's own IAM user instead of the new roles.",
      "Attach a service control policy (SCP) to the developers' accounts that denies iam:CreateRole. Have the security team create each role on request.",
      "Turn on IAM Access Analyzer to find roles that grant broad access. Delete any role that the analyzer reports after the developer creates it."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A condition on iam:CreateRole that requires the boundary means every role a developer creates is capped by that policy, whatever permissions the developer attaches to it.",
      "A boundary applies only to the entity that it is attached to. It would cap the developers' own users, but the roles that they create would still be uncapped.",
      "This stops developers from creating roles at all, and the security team would review every request, which the company wants to avoid.",
      "Access Analyzer reports after a role exists, so the broad role can be used before it is found and deleted."] },

{ id: "d1-08", d: 1,
  q: "A company runs Microsoft Active Directory in its on-premises data center and connects to AWS through AWS Direct Connect. The company plans to launch Amazon EC2 Windows instances that must join the existing domain. Employees must sign in with their current domain credentials, and the company does not want to store any directory data in AWS.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Deploy AD Connector in two subnets in the VPC. Point AD Connector at the on-premises domain controllers and join the instances to the domain through it.",
      "Deploy AWS Managed Microsoft AD in the VPC. Create a two-way forest trust with the on-premises domain and join the instances to the new domain.",
      "Deploy Simple AD in the VPC. Import the on-premises users into Simple AD and join the instances to the Simple AD domain.",
      "Use an Amazon Cognito user pool as the directory for the instances. Federate the user pool with the on-premises domain through SAML."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["AD Connector is a directory gateway that sends requests to the on-premises domain controllers without caching directory data in AWS. Instances join the existing domain, and employees use their current credentials.",
      "AWS Managed Microsoft AD is a separate directory that stores its own data in AWS. The instances would join the new domain instead of the existing one.",
      "Simple AD is a separate directory, and importing users copies directory data into AWS. Employees would also have separate accounts.",
      "Cognito user pools serve application sign-in. EC2 Windows instances cannot join a Cognito user pool as a domain."] },

{ id: "d1-09", d: 1,
  q: "A company is moving to a multi-account strategy on AWS. It needs a baseline that creates separate accounts for central logging and security auditing, applies preventive and detective controls to every account, and gives teams a standard way to request new accounts. The company has a small cloud team and wants to follow AWS best practices.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Set up an AWS Control Tower landing zone. Turn on the controls that the company needs and use Account Factory to create new accounts.",
      "Create an organization in AWS Organizations. Write AWS CloudFormation StackSets for the logging setup, the controls, and account creation, and maintain them in-house.",
      "Create an organization in AWS Organizations with SCPs for preventive controls. Deploy AWS Config conformance packs in each account for detective controls.",
      "Publish a product in AWS Service Catalog that creates accounts through the AWS Organizations API. Add AWS Config rules to the product for detective controls."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Control Tower sets up a landing zone with log archive and audit accounts, applies managed preventive and detective controls, and provides Account Factory for standard account requests. AWS maintains the building blocks.",
      "StackSets can build all of this, but the small team would design and maintain the whole baseline itself.",
      "This covers the controls, but it does not create the logging and audit accounts or a standard way to request accounts.",
      "This gives a way to request accounts, but the team would build and maintain the account creation logic and the shared accounts itself."] },

{ id: "d1-10", d: 1,
  q: "A financial services company stores reports in an Amazon S3 bucket. Applications in a VPC access the bucket through an S3 gateway endpoint. A new compliance rule states that the bucket must reject all requests that do not come through that specific endpoint, including requests from users who have valid IAM permissions.\n\nWhat should a solutions architect do to meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Add a bucket policy that denies all S3 actions when the aws:SourceVpce condition key does not match the ID of the gateway endpoint.",
      "Add an endpoint policy to the gateway endpoint that allows access only to the reports bucket. Keep the current bucket policy.",
      "Add a bucket policy that denies all S3 actions when the aws:SourceIp condition key does not match the CIDR range of the VPC.",
      "Remove the routes to the internet gateway from the VPC's route tables. Keep the gateway endpoint route in each private route table."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A deny statement in the bucket policy applies to every caller, whatever their IAM permissions. Checking aws:SourceVpce rejects any request that does not arrive through the named endpoint.",
      "An endpoint policy controls what traffic through the endpoint can reach. It does nothing to requests that reach the bucket from outside the VPC.",
      "Requests through a VPC endpoint carry private addresses that aws:SourceIp does not match, so this policy would block the applications themselves.",
      "Route changes affect only traffic from this VPC. Users outside the VPC could still reach the bucket over the internet."] },

{ id: "d1-11", d: 1,
  q: "A company runs application servers on Amazon EC2 instances in private subnets in two Availability Zones. The servers must download operating system updates from the internet over IPv4. The servers must not be reachable from the internet, and outbound access must stay available if one Availability Zone fails. Each zone already has a public subnet with a route to an internet gateway.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Deploy one NAT gateway for each Availability Zone, placed in a public subnet. Point each private subnet's default route at the NAT gateway in its own zone.",
      "Create one NAT gateway in a public subnet in one Availability Zone. Route the internet traffic from the private subnets in both zones to that NAT gateway.",
      "Create a NAT gateway in each private subnet. Route each private subnet's internet traffic to the NAT gateway in the same subnet.",
      "Attach an egress-only internet gateway to the VPC. Route the internet traffic from each private subnet to the egress-only internet gateway."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A NAT gateway lets private instances start outbound connections while blocking connections from the internet. One per zone, each with its own route, keeps outbound access working when a zone fails.",
      "This works until that zone fails. Then the servers in the other zone also lose their path to the internet.",
      "A NAT gateway needs a public subnet with a route to an internet gateway. In a private subnet it cannot reach the internet.",
      "An egress-only internet gateway handles outbound IPv6 traffic only. The servers download updates over IPv4."] },

{ id: "d1-12", d: 1,
  q: "An Application Load Balancer (ALB) sends a web application's traffic to Amazon EC2 instances that an Auto Scaling group manages. The IP addresses of the instances and the ALB nodes change often. The instances must accept HTTP traffic on port 8080 only from the ALB, and the rules must not need updates when addresses change.\n\nWhat should a solutions architect do to meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Add an inbound rule for port 8080 to the instances' security group with the ALB's security group as the source.",
      "Add an inbound rule for port 8080 to the instances' security group with the CIDR ranges of the ALB's subnets as the source.",
      "Add an inbound rule for port 8080 to the network ACL of the instances' subnets from the ALB's subnets. Allow return traffic on ephemeral ports.",
      "Add an inbound rule for port 8080 to the instances' security group for each current ALB node IP address. Update the rule from an AWS Lambda function."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A security group rule can reference another security group. Any traffic from resources in the ALB's security group is allowed, whatever their addresses, so the rule never needs updates.",
      "Subnet ranges would also allow any other resource in those subnets, not only the ALB.",
      "Network ACLs work at the subnet level with address ranges, so they also allow other resources in the ALB's subnets.",
      "This needs constant updates and custom code, and traffic fails between an address change and the next update."] },

{ id: "d1-13", d: 1,
  q: "A company's web servers run on Amazon EC2 instances in a public subnet. The security team has found an IP address range that is sending malicious traffic to the servers. The team must block all traffic from that range to every instance in the subnet immediately, while all other internet users keep their access.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["In the subnet's network ACL, deny inbound traffic from the IP range in a rule numbered lower than the rule that allows web traffic.",
      "In the web servers' security group, deny inbound traffic from the IP range. Keep the rule that allows web traffic from 0.0.0.0/0.",
      "In the subnet's network ACL, deny inbound traffic from the IP range in a rule numbered higher than the rule that allows web traffic.",
      "Remove the security group rule that allows web traffic from 0.0.0.0/0. Add allow rules for the IP ranges that the company's customers use."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Network ACLs support deny rules and evaluate rules in order, starting with the lowest number. A deny rule numbered below the allow rule blocks the range for every instance in the subnet.",
      "Security groups support allow rules only, so a deny rule cannot be added.",
      "The allow rule matches the traffic first and is applied, so the deny rule with the higher number is never reached.",
      "This blocks every internet user who is not on the list, which breaks access for other users."] },

{ id: "d1-14", d: 1,
  q: "A company's web application runs behind an Application Load Balancer (ALB). A penetration test found a SQL injection flaw in how the application handles query strings. The development team needs several weeks to fix the code. The company needs to block SQL injection attempts before they reach the application as soon as possible.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create an AWS WAF web ACL that uses the AWS managed SQL database rule group. Associate the web ACL with the ALB.",
      "Create a web ACL in AWS WAF that limits the number of requests from each IP address. Associate the web ACL with the ALB.",
      "Subscribe to AWS Shield Advanced for the ALB. Request that the Shield Response Team add rules that block SQL injection.",
      "Create an AWS WAF web ACL that uses the AWS managed SQL database rule group. Associate the web ACL with the VPC that contains the ALB."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The SQL database managed rule group inspects requests for SQL injection patterns. AWS WAF on the ALB blocks matching requests before they reach the application.",
      "A rate-based rule limits how many requests an address sends. A single SQL injection request is still allowed through.",
      "Shield Advanced protects against DDoS attacks. It does not inspect requests for SQL injection on its own.",
      "A web ACL is associated with resources such as an ALB, API Gateway, or CloudFront. It cannot be associated with a VPC."] },

{ id: "d1-15", d: 1,
  q: "A media company runs a public website on Amazon CloudFront with an Application Load Balancer (ALB) origin. Large DDoS attacks hit the site during two product launches, and the scaling during the attacks increased the AWS bill. The company wants 24/7 help from AWS DDoS experts during attacks and protection from scaling charges that DDoS attacks cause.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Subscribe to AWS Shield Advanced. Add the CloudFront distribution and the ALB as protected resources.",
      "Rely on AWS Shield Standard, which already protects CloudFront. Add AWS WAF rate-based rules to the distribution.",
      "Subscribe to AWS Shield Advanced. Add only the ALB as a protected resource and keep the distribution on AWS Shield Standard.",
      "Add AWS WAF rate-based rules to the distribution and the ALB. Use AWS Firewall Manager to apply the rules to all accounts."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Shield Advanced adds access to the Shield Response Team and cost protection for scaling charges caused by DDoS attacks on protected resources. Protecting both the distribution and the ALB covers the whole path.",
      "Shield Standard gives baseline protection at no cost, but it includes neither the response team nor cost protection.",
      "Cost protection applies only to protected resources, so CloudFront charges from an attack on the distribution would not be covered.",
      "Rate-based rules can slow some attacks, but they add neither expert help nor protection from scaling charges."] },

{ id: "d1-16", d: 1,
  q: "A company's login API runs on Amazon API Gateway. The security team sees credential stuffing attacks in which single IP addresses send thousands of login requests in a few minutes. Normal users send fewer than 20 login requests in 5 minutes. The company must automatically block any IP address that goes over a set request rate.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Protect the API stage with an AWS WAF web ACL. Add a rate-based rule that is scoped to the login path and blocks IP addresses over the limit.",
      "Configure an API Gateway usage plan with a throttle limit for the login path. Give each user an API key to include in each request.",
      "Protect the API stage with an AWS WAF web ACL. Add an IP set rule that blocks the IP addresses that appeared in the previous day's access logs.",
      "Turn on Amazon GuardDuty for the account. Configure GuardDuty to block IP addresses that send large numbers of login requests."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A rate-based rule counts requests from each IP address over a time window and blocks addresses that pass the limit, then lets them through again when the rate drops. The scope keeps other paths unaffected.",
      "Usage plans throttle by API key. Attackers would not send a valid key for each request, and throttling is not blocking by IP address.",
      "An IP set built from old logs is static and always behind. New attacking addresses would get through.",
      "GuardDuty reports threats but does not block traffic to API Gateway."] },

{ id: "d1-17", d: 1,
  q: "An application on Amazon EC2 connects to a MySQL database on Amazon RDS. The database password is stored in a configuration file on each instance. A new policy requires the password to change every 30 days without application downtime, and the application must always read the current password at run time.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Move the password into AWS Secrets Manager with automatic rotation every 30 days. Update the application to read the secret at run time.",
      "Store the password as a SecureString parameter in AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store. Update the application to read the parameter at run time.",
      "Turn on automatic rotation for the AWS KMS key that encrypts the DB instance. Update the application to decrypt the password with that key.",
      "Store the password in AWS Secrets Manager. Create an Amazon EventBridge schedule that runs a custom AWS Lambda function to change the password every 30 days."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Secrets Manager rotates RDS credentials on a schedule with a rotation function that AWS provides, and it updates the database and the secret together. The application always reads the current value.",
      "Parameter Store keeps the value encrypted, but it has no built-in rotation, so someone would still change the password.",
      "Rotating a KMS key changes the key material that encrypts data. It does not change the database password.",
      "A custom function and schedule repeat what Secrets Manager rotation already does, and the team would maintain the code."] },

{ id: "d1-18", d: 1,
  q: "A company's security team suspects that some Amazon EC2 instances are communicating with known cryptocurrency mining servers. The team wants a managed service that analyzes VPC flow logs, DNS logs, and AWS CloudTrail events across all accounts in its organization and reports this kind of activity, without installing agents. The security team must review the findings for all accounts from one account.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Turn on Amazon GuardDuty in all accounts with a delegated administrator account. Review the findings in the delegated administrator account.",
      "Turn on Amazon Inspector in all accounts with a delegated administrator account. Review the findings in the delegated administrator account.",
      "Turn on Amazon Macie in all accounts with a delegated administrator account. Review the findings in the delegated administrator account.",
      "Turn on Amazon GuardDuty in each account as a standalone account. Have each account's team review its own findings."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["GuardDuty analyzes flow logs, DNS logs, and CloudTrail events with threat intelligence and reports findings such as communication with mining pools. A delegated administrator sees findings from every account.",
      "Amazon Inspector scans workloads for software vulnerabilities and network exposure. It does not detect active communication with malicious servers.",
      "Macie discovers sensitive data in Amazon S3. It does not analyze network or account activity for threats.",
      "GuardDuty would detect the activity, but the findings would stay in each account instead of in one place for the security team."] },

{ id: "d1-19", d: 1,
  q: "A company stores customer files in hundreds of Amazon S3 buckets across several accounts in its organization. A new privacy regulation requires the company to find which buckets contain personally identifiable information (PII), such as passport and payment card numbers, and to keep finding new PII as the data changes.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Turn on Amazon Macie for the organization. Turn on automated sensitive data discovery for the S3 buckets.",
      "Turn on Amazon GuardDuty S3 Protection for the organization. Review the findings for buckets that store PII.",
      "Turn on Amazon Macie for the organization. Run a one-time sensitive data discovery job on the current buckets.",
      "Query each bucket with Amazon Athena by using regular expressions for PII patterns. Run the queries each week from AWS Step Functions."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Macie identifies PII such as passport and payment card numbers with managed data identifiers. Automated discovery keeps sampling the buckets, so new PII is found as data changes.",
      "GuardDuty S3 Protection looks for suspicious access to buckets. It does not classify the data in them.",
      "A one-time job finds the PII that exists today, but it does not keep finding PII as data changes.",
      "This is custom code that needs ongoing tuning, and Athena reads only data in formats that it can query."] },

{ id: "d1-20", d: 1,
  q: "A company needs a private, encrypted connection between its office network and a VPC within the next week. Traffic between the office and AWS is about 50 Mbps during business hours. The office already has a reliable internet connection and a firewall that supports IPsec. The company will review a dedicated connection again if traffic grows.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements MOST cost-effectively?",
  o: ["Use AWS Site-to-Site VPN between a virtual private gateway on the VPC and the office firewall, which acts as the customer gateway.",
      "Order a 1 Gbps AWS Direct Connect dedicated connection. Create a private virtual interface to a virtual private gateway on the VPC.",
      "Create an AWS Client VPN endpoint in the VPC. Install the Client VPN software on each office computer that needs access to the VPC.",
      "Order a 50 Mbps AWS Direct Connect hosted connection from an AWS Direct Connect Partner. Run a Site-to-Site VPN connection over the hosted connection."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Site-to-Site VPN uses the existing internet connection and firewall, encrypts traffic with IPsec, and can be set up in hours. It costs far less than Direct Connect for this amount of traffic.",
      "A dedicated connection takes weeks or longer to deliver and costs much more. Direct Connect alone also does not encrypt traffic.",
      "Client VPN connects individual devices, not a network, and it charges for each connection. It is a poor fit for an office network.",
      "A hosted connection still takes time to provision through a partner and adds port and partner costs that the VPN alone avoids."] },

{ id: "d1-21", d: 1,
  q: "A company's data center reaches a VPC over AWS Direct Connect through a private virtual interface. A new regulation requires all traffic between the data center and the VPC to be encrypted in transit. The company wants to keep the consistent network performance that Direct Connect provides. The VPC uses a virtual private gateway.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create a public virtual interface on the Direct Connect connection. Run an AWS Site-to-Site VPN connection over it to the virtual private gateway.",
      "Turn on encryption for the private virtual interface in the Direct Connect console. Keep the existing routes to the virtual private gateway.",
      "Replace the private virtual interface with AWS Site-to-Site VPN tunnels over the internet to the virtual private gateway after a cutover.",
      "Create an AWS Client VPN endpoint for each server in the data center. Route the Client VPN traffic over the private virtual interface."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An IPsec VPN that runs over Direct Connect encrypts the traffic while it still travels on the dedicated connection, so the company keeps consistent performance.",
      "A private virtual interface has no encryption setting. Direct Connect traffic is not encrypted by default.",
      "This encrypts the traffic, but it moves it to the internet, so the company loses the consistent performance of Direct Connect.",
      "Client VPN is for individual users and devices. Running it for every server is impractical and does not fit this design."] },

{ id: "d1-22", d: 1,
  q: "Amazon EC2 instances in private subnets call the Amazon SQS API. The subnets have no route to the internet, and the company does not want to add a NAT gateway. The application uses the default SQS endpoint name and must work without any code changes. DNS resolution and DNS hostnames are turned on for the VPC.\n\nWhat should a solutions architect do to meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create an interface VPC endpoint for Amazon SQS in the private subnets. Turn on private DNS for the endpoint.",
      "Create a gateway VPC endpoint for Amazon SQS. Add the endpoint to the route tables of the private subnets.",
      "Create an interface VPC endpoint for Amazon SQS in the private subnets. Change the application to call the endpoint-specific DNS name.",
      "Create an egress-only internet gateway for the VPC. Route the SQS traffic from the private subnets to the egress-only internet gateway."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An interface endpoint gives SQS private addresses in the subnets. With private DNS, the default SQS endpoint name resolves to those addresses, so the code does not change.",
      "Gateway endpoints exist only for Amazon S3 and DynamoDB. SQS needs an interface endpoint.",
      "This reaches SQS privately, but the application would need a code or configuration change, which the company does not want.",
      "An egress-only internet gateway carries IPv6 traffic to the internet. The company does not want internet access for these subnets."] },

{ id: "d1-23", d: 1,
  q: "A software company offers an API to customers that run workloads in their own AWS accounts. In the company's VPC, a Network Load Balancer (NLB) fronts the Amazon EC2 instances that serve the API. Customers want private access without using the internet, and many customer VPCs use CIDR ranges that overlap with the company's VPC.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create a VPC endpoint service that uses the NLB. Let customers create interface VPC endpoints for the service in their own VPCs.",
      "Create a VPC peering connection with each customer VPC. Add routes to the API subnets in the route tables of each peered VPC.",
      "Attach the company VPC and each customer VPC to an AWS Transit Gateway. Add routes for the API subnets to the route tables of each VPC.",
      "Put the NLB behind an Amazon CloudFront distribution with a custom domain. Restrict the distribution to the IP addresses of the customers."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["AWS PrivateLink exposes the service through an interface endpoint in each customer VPC. Traffic stays on the AWS network, and overlapping CIDR ranges do not matter because no routing between the VPCs is needed.",
      "VPC peering cannot connect VPCs with overlapping CIDR ranges, and it would open routing between whole networks.",
      "Transit gateway routing also breaks with overlapping CIDR ranges and connects whole networks instead of one service.",
      "CloudFront serves traffic over the internet, which the customers do not want."] },

{ id: "d1-24", d: 1,
  q: "A company's administrators connect to Linux Amazon EC2 instances in private subnets through a bastion host that allows SSH from the office IP range. The company wants to remove the bastion host, close all inbound ports on the instances, and record every session for audit. The administrators already sign in to AWS through IAM Identity Center.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements MOST securely?",
  o: ["Use AWS Systems Manager Session Manager with the SSM Agent and an instance profile. Send the session logs to Amazon S3.",
      "Use EC2 Instance Connect to push temporary SSH keys. Allow inbound SSH on the instances from the office IP range.",
      "Move the bastion host to a private subnet behind a Network Load Balancer. Allow inbound SSH on the instances from the bastion host only.",
      "Use AWS Systems Manager Session Manager with the SSM Agent and an instance profile. Keep inbound SSH open from the office IP range as a fallback."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Session Manager connects through the SSM Agent's outbound connection, so the instances need no inbound ports and no bastion host. IAM controls access, and session activity can be logged to S3.",
      "Temporary keys help, but inbound SSH stays open, which the company wants to close.",
      "The bastion host remains, and the instances still accept inbound SSH.",
      "Session Manager is the right tool, but leaving SSH open keeps an inbound port that the company wants closed."] },

{ id: "d1-25", d: 1,
  q: "A company serves static assets from an Amazon S3 bucket through an Amazon CloudFront distribution. Users have found that they can also download the files from the S3 bucket URL, which bypasses the distribution's geographic restrictions. The objects are encrypted with SSE-KMS. The files must be available only through CloudFront. The distribution already uses the bucket as its origin.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Configure origin access control (OAC) on the distribution. Allow only the CloudFront service principal for that distribution in the bucket policy and the KMS key policy.",
      "Configure an origin access identity (OAI) on the distribution. Change the bucket policy to allow only the OAI to read objects.",
      "Use the S3 static website endpoint as a custom origin. Add a bucket policy that allows only requests with a secret Referer header.",
      "Configure origin access control (OAC) on the distribution. Keep the current public bucket policy and add a geographic restriction."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["OAC signs CloudFront's requests to S3 and supports objects encrypted with SSE-KMS. Allowing only the distribution in the bucket and key policies blocks direct access to the bucket.",
      "An OAI does not support objects that are encrypted with SSE-KMS, so CloudFront could not read the files.",
      "S3 website endpoints do not serve SSE-KMS encrypted objects, and a Referer header is easy to copy.",
      "The public bucket policy still lets anyone download directly from S3, so the bypass remains."] },

{ id: "d1-26", d: 1,
  q: "A training company sells video courses. Each course has hundreds of video segment files in Amazon S3 that are delivered through Amazon CloudFront. Only customers who bought a course may watch it. The URLs that the video player requests for each segment must stay the same. Customers watch the courses on the company's website after they sign in.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Issue CloudFront signed cookies for the course path after a customer signs in. Require signed requests on the cache behavior for the courses.",
      "Issue CloudFront signed URLs for each segment file after a customer signs in. Require signed requests on the cache behavior for the courses.",
      "Issue S3 presigned URLs for each segment file after a customer signs in. Keep the CloudFront distribution in front of the bucket.",
      "Add an AWS WAF rule to the distribution that allows only the IP address that each customer used when the customer signed in."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Signed cookies grant access to many files under a path without changing their URLs, which suits players that request many segments.",
      "Signed URLs work, but every segment URL would change to include a signature, which the company does not want.",
      "Presigned URLs go to S3 directly instead of through CloudFront, and they also change every segment URL.",
      "IP addresses change and are shared behind networks, so this would block buyers and allow others."] },

{ id: "d1-27", d: 1,
  q: "A company collects application logs from 30 AWS accounts in its organization in AWS Organizations into one Amazon S3 bucket in a central logging account. New accounts join every month. Only principals from accounts in the organization may write to the bucket, and the bucket policy must not need changes when accounts join.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Add a bucket policy statement that allows s3:PutObject when the aws:PrincipalOrgID condition key matches the organization ID.",
      "Add a bucket policy statement that allows s3:PutObject for each account ID. Add new account IDs to the statement as accounts join.",
      "Add a bucket policy statement that allows s3:PutObject for all principals. Turn on S3 Object Ownership to take ownership of the uploaded logs.",
      "Create an IAM role in the logging account that can write to the bucket. Add each new account ID to the role's trust policy as accounts join."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["aws:PrincipalOrgID matches any principal from an account in the organization, so new accounts can write without policy changes, and principals outside the organization are refused.",
      "Listing account IDs works, but the policy must change every month, which the company wants to avoid.",
      "This lets anyone write to the bucket. Object Ownership controls who owns objects, not who can upload them.",
      "The trust policy would need an update for every new account."] },

{ id: "d1-28", d: 1,
  q: "A company has many project teams that share one AWS account. Each team must be able to manage only the Amazon EC2 instances that belong to its project. The company creates new projects every week and wants to avoid writing a new IAM policy for each project. All instances and IAM roles already have a Project tag.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements in the MOST operationally efficient way?",
  o: ["Create one IAM policy that allows EC2 actions when the instance's Project tag matches the Project tag of the calling role. Attach it to all team roles.",
      "Create an IAM policy for each project that lists the instance IDs of that project. Attach each policy to the role of the matching team.",
      "Create a separate VPC for each project and launch the project's instances in it. Grant each team's role EC2 permissions in its own VPC.",
      "Create a service control policy (SCP) for each project that denies actions on other projects' instances. Attach the SCPs to the account."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Attribute-based access control compares the resource tag with the principal tag in one policy. New projects need only tags, not new policies.",
      "This needs a new policy for each project and an update each time instances change.",
      "A new VPC for each project is heavy, and many EC2 permissions cannot be scoped to a VPC.",
      "A new SCP for each project is still a policy per project, and an account can have only a few SCPs attached."] },

{ id: "d1-29", d: 1,
  q: "A European company must keep all workloads in the eu-west-1 and eu-central-1 Regions for data residency. It uses AWS Organizations with many member accounts. Users must not be able to create resources in any other Region, but global services such as IAM must keep working. The rule must also apply to administrators in the member accounts.\n\nWhat should a solutions architect do to meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Attach an SCP that denies actions outside the two Regions by using the aws:RequestedRegion condition key, with an exception for global services.",
      "Attach an SCP that denies actions outside the two Regions by using the aws:RequestedRegion condition key, with no exception for any service.",
      "Attach an IAM policy to each user and role that allows actions only in the two Regions. Add the policy to each new account.",
      "Turn off all other Regions in the account settings of each member account. Turn a Region back on when a team requests it."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The SCP blocks requests to other Regions in every member account. Global services such as IAM send their requests through us-east-1, so they need an exception to keep working.",
      "Without an exception, calls to global services that are served from us-east-1 would be denied, which breaks IAM.",
      "Administrators in each account could change these policies, so users could still reach other Regions. It is also more work.",
      "Regions that are turned on by default cannot be turned off, so this does not block them."] },

{ id: "d1-30", d: 1,
  q: "A company is building a REST API on Amazon API Gateway with AWS Lambda backends for a customer web app. Customers must sign up and sign in with an email address, and some customers want to use MFA. Only signed-in customers may call the API. The company does not want to build or run its own user database.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Create an Amazon Cognito user pool with optional MFA. Add a Cognito user pool authorizer to the API methods.",
      "Create an Amazon Cognito identity pool that allows unauthenticated identities. Use IAM authorization on the API methods for all callers.",
      "Store users and hashed passwords in an Amazon DynamoDB table. Add a Lambda authorizer that checks the credentials on each request.",
      "Create an API key for each customer in API Gateway. Require the key on each API method and send the key to each customer after sign-up."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A user pool handles sign-up, sign-in, and MFA as a managed directory. The user pool authorizer validates tokens on each call, so only signed-in customers reach the API.",
      "Unauthenticated identities let anyone get credentials, so callers who have not signed in could call the API.",
      "This builds and runs a user database and authentication code, which the company wants to avoid.",
      "API keys identify clients for usage plans. They do not sign users in and are not a secure way to authorize callers."] },

{ id: "d1-31", d: 1,
  q: "A company stores millions of small objects in an Amazon S3 bucket that uses SSE-KMS with a customer managed key. AWS KMS request charges have grown sharply as the number of uploads and downloads has increased. The company must keep using the same KMS key for the objects. The bucket receives about 50 million requests each day.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements MOST cost-effectively?",
  o: ["Turn on S3 Bucket Keys in the bucket's default encryption settings.",
      "Change the bucket's default encryption to SSE-S3 and use the KMS key only for new buckets. Keep the existing objects under the KMS key.",
      "Request a higher AWS KMS request quota for the account to allow more requests at a lower rate.",
      "Switch the bucket's default encryption to DSSE-KMS, which adds a second layer of encryption. Use the same KMS key for both layers."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An S3 Bucket Key is a bucket-level key that S3 derives from the KMS key. S3 then calls AWS KMS far less often, which cuts request costs while the same KMS key protects the data.",
      "SSE-S3 removes the KMS charges, but the objects would no longer be protected by the company's KMS key.",
      "A quota increase lets the account make more requests. It does not lower the price of each request.",
      "DSSE-KMS applies two layers of encryption and makes more KMS requests, so the cost would go up."] },

{ id: "d1-32", d: 1,
  q: "A company's PostgreSQL database on Amazon RDS was created without encryption. A new security standard requires all databases to be encrypted at rest with an AWS KMS key. The company can accept a short maintenance window for the change. The application's connection string can be updated during that window.\n\nWhat should a solutions architect do to meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Take an RDS snapshot of the DB instance. Copy the snapshot with encryption turned on, and restore a new DB instance from the encrypted copy.",
      "Modify the RDS DB instance to turn on encryption at rest. Apply the change during the next scheduled maintenance window.",
      "Create an encrypted RDS read replica of the DB instance. Promote the read replica to a standalone DB instance after it catches up.",
      "Turn on Amazon EBS encryption by default for the Region. Reboot the DB instance to encrypt its storage volumes."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Encryption cannot be turned on for an existing unencrypted DB instance. Copying a snapshot with encryption and restoring from the copy creates an encrypted DB instance with the same data.",
      "RDS does not allow encryption to be added to an existing unencrypted DB instance through a modification.",
      "A read replica of an unencrypted DB instance cannot be encrypted, so this does not produce an encrypted database.",
      "EBS encryption by default applies to EC2 volumes that are created later. It does not encrypt RDS storage."] },

{ id: "d1-33", d: 1,
  q: "A company's developers launch Amazon EC2 instances from many different AMIs and launch templates in one AWS Region. An audit found several unencrypted Amazon EBS volumes. Every new EBS volume and snapshot copy in the Region must be encrypted, even when a developer forgets to ask for encryption. Developers must still be able to create volumes themselves.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Turn on EBS encryption by default for the Region to ensure that every new volume is encrypted.",
      "Add an EBS encryption setting to the block device mappings in every launch template and AMI. Review new templates each week.",
      "Create an AWS Config rule that finds unencrypted EBS volumes. Have an AWS Lambda function snapshot, encrypt, and replace each volume that the rule finds.",
      "Create an SCP that denies ec2:CreateVolume to all developers. Let a central team create encrypted volumes for developers on request."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Encryption by default is one setting for each Region. After it is on, every new volume and snapshot copy is encrypted, whatever the developer requests.",
      "This depends on every template being correct, and the weekly review would miss volumes created in between.",
      "This reacts after unencrypted volumes exist and replaces volumes in place, which is complex and disruptive.",
      "This blocks developers and adds a manual request process for every volume."] },

{ id: "d1-34", d: 1,
  q: "A company uses a customer managed AWS KMS key to encrypt sensitive data. The security team must manage the key, including its key policy and rotation, but must not be able to encrypt or decrypt data with it. An application role must be able to encrypt and decrypt data but must not be able to change the key.\n\nWhat should a solutions architect do to meet these requirements?",
  o: ["In the key policy, give the security team's role the key administration actions only. Give the application role only the cryptographic actions that it uses.",
      "In the key policy, give both roles kms:* on the key. Turn on AWS CloudTrail logging to review how each role uses the key.",
      "In the key policy, give the application role the key administration actions and the cryptographic actions. Give the security team read-only access.",
      "Create one KMS key for the security team to manage. Give the application role a second key that has no key administrators."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Key policies can separate key administrators from key users. Administrators manage the key but cannot use it, and the application can use it but not change it.",
      "CloudTrail records use after it happens. Both roles would still be able to do everything with the key.",
      "The application role could then change the key policy, and the security team could no longer manage the key.",
      "The security team would not manage the key that protects the data, and a key without administrators is hard to maintain."] },

{ id: "d1-35", d: 1,
  q: "A payments company must keep its encryption keys in single-tenant hardware security modules (HSMs) that only the company controls. AWS must not have access to the key material. The company's applications use the standard PKCS #11 library. The HSMs must run in the company's own VPC and stay available if one Availability Zone fails.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create an AWS CloudHSM cluster with HSMs in two Availability Zones. Connect the applications to the cluster through the PKCS #11 library.",
      "Create AWS KMS customer managed keys with key material that AWS KMS generates. Restrict the key policies to the application roles.",
      "Import the company's own key material into AWS KMS customer managed keys. Delete the local copy of the key material after the import.",
      "Create an AWS CloudHSM cluster with one HSM in a single Availability Zone. Connect the applications through the PKCS #11 library."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["CloudHSM gives the company dedicated HSMs in its VPC that it fully controls, and it supports the PKCS #11 library. HSMs in two zones keep the cluster available.",
      "AWS KMS runs on HSMs that AWS operates for many customers. It does not give single-tenant HSMs that only the company controls.",
      "Imported key material is still used inside AWS KMS, which is not a single-tenant HSM controlled by the company.",
      "A cluster with one HSM in one zone is a single point of failure, and the HSMs must stay available if a zone fails."] },

{ id: "d1-36", d: 1,
  q: "A company runs several public websites behind Application Load Balancers (ALBs). Its TLS certificates come from a third-party certificate authority, and twice last year a certificate expired because a renewal was missed. The company wants certificates that renew without any action from its staff. The company's domain names are hosted in Amazon Route 53.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Request public certificates from AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) with DNS validation. Attach the certificates to the ALB HTTPS listeners.",
      "Import the third-party certificates into ACM to provide a single place to manage them. Attach the imported certificates to the ALB HTTPS listeners.",
      "Request public certificates from ACM with email validation. Attach the certificates to the ALB HTTPS listeners.",
      "Create a certificate authority in AWS Private CA. Issue the website certificates from it and attach them to the ALB HTTPS listeners."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["ACM renews public certificates that use DNS validation automatically while the validation record stays in place, and the ALB picks up the renewed certificate.",
      "ACM does not renew imported certificates. Someone would still renew each one with the third-party authority and import it again.",
      "Email-validated certificates need someone to approve a renewal email, so staff action is still required.",
      "Browsers do not trust certificates from a private certificate authority, so public visitors would see errors."] },

{ id: "d1-37", d: 1,
  q: "A company hosts an application in the eu-west-2 Region behind an Application Load Balancer. The company is adding an Amazon CloudFront distribution with the custom domain name www.example.com. A public certificate for the domain was requested from AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) in eu-west-2, but the certificate does not appear when the team edits the distribution.\n\nWhat should a solutions architect do to meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Request a certificate for the domain from ACM in the US East (N. Virginia) Region. Choose that certificate in the distribution settings.",
      "Wait for ACM to replicate the eu-west-2 certificate to the other Regions. Choose the certificate in the distribution settings after replication finishes.",
      "Attach the eu-west-2 certificate to the ALB. Configure CloudFront to pass the viewer's TLS connection through to the ALB unchanged.",
      "Request a certificate for the domain from ACM in the Region nearest to most users. Choose that certificate in the distribution settings."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["CloudFront uses ACM certificates only from the US East (N. Virginia) Region. A certificate requested there appears in the distribution settings.",
      "ACM does not replicate certificates between Regions, so the certificate would never appear.",
      "CloudFront ends the viewer's TLS connection at the edge. It needs its own certificate for the custom domain.",
      "The Region nearest to users does not matter. CloudFront looks for the certificate only in US East (N. Virginia)."] },

{ id: "d1-38", d: 1,
  q: "A company's security policy requires all access to its Amazon S3 buckets to use encrypted connections. A scan shows that some older clients still upload data to one bucket over HTTP. The company must reject these requests at the bucket itself instead of waiting for every client to be updated.\n\nWhat should a solutions architect do to meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Add a bucket policy statement that denies all S3 actions when the aws:SecureTransport condition key is false.",
      "Turn on default encryption for the bucket with SSE-S3. Apply it to the existing objects with S3 Batch Operations.",
      "Add a bucket policy statement that denies uploads that do not include the x-amz-server-side-encryption request header.",
      "Turn on S3 Transfer Acceleration for the bucket and give the older clients the accelerated endpoint name to use."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["aws:SecureTransport is false for requests over plain HTTP. The deny statement rejects those requests, so every client must use HTTPS.",
      "Default encryption protects data at rest. It does not stop clients from sending data over HTTP.",
      "That header requests encryption at rest. It says nothing about whether the connection is encrypted.",
      "Transfer Acceleration speeds up long-distance transfers. It does not reject HTTP requests."] },

{ id: "d1-39", d: 1,
  q: "A brokerage firm must store trade records in Amazon S3 for 7 years in a write once, read many (WORM) format. No user, including the root user of the account, may delete or overwrite a record during that period. Auditors also need proof that an administrator cannot remove the protection.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Turn on S3 Object Lock for the bucket. Store the records with a 7-year retention period in compliance mode.",
      "Turn on S3 Object Lock for the bucket. Store the records with a 7-year retention period in governance mode.",
      "Turn on S3 Versioning and MFA delete for the bucket. Add a bucket policy that denies s3:DeleteObject to all users.",
      "Add an S3 Lifecycle rule that moves the records to S3 Glacier Deep Archive after 1 day and expires them after 7 years."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["In compliance mode, no user, including the root user, can delete a locked object version or shorten its retention until the period ends.",
      "Users with the s3:BypassGovernanceRetention permission can remove governance mode protection, so it is not strict WORM.",
      "The root user or an administrator could change the bucket policy, so records could still be deleted.",
      "Lifecycle rules manage storage class and expiration. Records in Deep Archive can still be deleted at any time."] },

{ id: "d1-40", d: 1,
  q: "A company stores design files in an Amazon S3 bucket. Users sometimes overwrite or delete files by mistake. The company must be able to restore any earlier version of a file for 90 days after it changes, and storage costs must not keep growing with old versions. The bucket holds about 40 TB, and files change many times each day.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Turn on S3 Versioning for the bucket.",
      "Add a lifecycle rule that permanently deletes noncurrent versions 90 days after they become noncurrent.",
      "Lock every object for 90 days with S3 Object Lock in compliance mode. Let users read every stored version.",
      "Add a lifecycle rule that expires current object versions 90 days after they are created.",
      "Turn on S3 Cross-Region Replication to a second bucket in another Region."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Versioning keeps the earlier version of an object when it is overwritten or deleted, so users can restore it.",
      "Removing noncurrent versions after 90 days keeps the recovery window that the company needs and stops old versions from piling up.",
      "Compliance mode would stop users from deleting files on purpose too, and it does not remove old versions after 90 days.",
      "This would delete the files that users are still working on after 90 days.",
      "Replication copies the data to another Region and doubles storage cost. It does not give a 90-day version history on its own."] },

{ id: "d1-41", d: 1,
  q: "A company runs Amazon RDS databases, Amazon EBS volumes, and Amazon DynamoDB tables in us-east-1. For disaster recovery, auditors require daily backups of all three, kept for 35 days, with a copy of each backup in us-west-2. The company wants one place to manage and monitor these backups. Every resource that needs protection carries a Backup tag.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Create an AWS Backup plan with a daily rule and 35-day retention. Add a copy action to a backup vault in us-west-2 and assign the resources by tag.",
      "Use RDS automated backups, Amazon Data Lifecycle Manager policies for the volumes, and DynamoDB point-in-time recovery. Copy each type of backup to us-west-2 with a scheduled script.",
      "Create an AWS Backup plan in us-east-1 with a daily rule and 35-day retention. Turn on S3 Cross-Region Replication for the backup vault.",
      "Create an AWS Backup plan in us-west-2 with 35-day retention that backs up the us-east-1 resources directly each day."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["AWS Backup covers RDS, EBS, and DynamoDB in one plan, and a copy action sends each backup to a vault in another Region. One console shows the status of every job.",
      "This uses three different tools and a custom script, so there is no single place to manage and monitor the backups.",
      "A backup vault is not an S3 bucket, so S3 replication settings do not apply to it. The copy action is the cross-Region feature.",
      "A backup plan protects resources in its own Region. Copies to another Region are made with a copy action."] },

{ id: "d1-42", d: 1,
  q: "A healthcare company uses AWS Backup to protect its databases. After a ransomware incident at another company, the security team requires that nobody, including administrators and the root user, can delete backups before their retention period ends or shorten that period. The vault holds backups of the production databases from several accounts.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Apply AWS Backup Vault Lock in compliance mode to the backup vault. Set the minimum retention to match the backup plan.",
      "Apply AWS Backup Vault Lock in governance mode to the backup vault. Set the minimum retention to match the backup plan.",
      "Add an access policy to the backup vault that denies backup:DeleteRecoveryPoint to every IAM user and role in the account.",
      "Encrypt the backup vault with a customer managed AWS KMS key. Remove the kms:Decrypt permission from all administrators."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["After the grace period, a vault locked in compliance mode cannot be changed or deleted by anyone, including the root user, and recovery points cannot be deleted early.",
      "Users with enough IAM permissions can remove a governance mode lock, so administrators could still delete backups.",
      "An administrator or the root user could change or remove the vault access policy.",
      "Removing decrypt access does not stop anyone from deleting the backups."] },

{ id: "d1-43", d: 1,
  q: "A company encrypts data in Amazon S3 and Amazon EBS with a customer managed AWS KMS key. A compliance standard requires the key material to change at least once a year. Data that was encrypted before a change must stay readable, and applications must not need changes. About 30 applications in the account use the key.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Turn on automatic key rotation for the customer managed KMS key.",
      "Create a new customer managed KMS key each year. Re-encrypt all existing data with the new key and delete the old key.",
      "Create a new customer managed KMS key each year and point the key alias at it. Schedule the old key for deletion after 30 days.",
      "Turn on S3 Bucket Keys for each bucket, which creates a new bucket-level key from the KMS key each day."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Automatic rotation creates new key material on a schedule and keeps the older material, so AWS KMS can still decrypt data that was encrypted before. The key ID and alias stay the same.",
      "Re-encrypting all data each year is a large manual job, and any data that is missed becomes unreadable after the old key is deleted.",
      "Data encrypted under the old key becomes unreadable when that key is deleted.",
      "Bucket Keys reduce KMS requests. They do not rotate the KMS key material and do not cover EBS."] },

{ id: "d1-44", d: 1,
  q: "An application encrypts records with AWS KMS before it stores them in an Amazon DynamoDB global table that replicates between us-east-1 and eu-west-1. Applications in each Region must decrypt any record locally, even when us-east-1 is unavailable. The records hold payment data, so the keys must stay in AWS KMS.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create a KMS multi-Region primary key in us-east-1 and a replica key in eu-west-1. Have each application use the key in its own Region.",
      "Create a single-Region customer managed KMS key in us-east-1. Have the applications in both Regions call that key in us-east-1 to decrypt records.",
      "Create a separate single-Region customer managed KMS key in each Region. Have each application use the key in its own Region.",
      "Create a single-Region key in each Region with the same alias name. Use the shared alias to decrypt records from either Region."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Multi-Region keys share the same key ID and key material, so a record encrypted in one Region can be decrypted with the related key in the other Region without a cross-Region call.",
      "The eu-west-1 application could not decrypt anything when us-east-1 is unavailable.",
      "Separate keys have different key material, so neither Region could decrypt records that were encrypted in the other Region.",
      "An alias is only a name. Keys with the same alias in two Regions still have different key material."] },

{ id: "d1-45", d: 1,
  q: "A company has 60 AWS accounts in AWS Organizations. After a data leak from a public bucket, the security team requires that no Amazon S3 bucket or object in any account can be made public. Account administrators must not be able to change this protection. The security team has a dedicated IAM role that it uses to manage these settings.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Turn on S3 Block Public Access at the account level in every account.",
      "Attach an SCP that denies s3:PutAccountPublicAccessBlock to all principals except a security team role.",
      "Turn on default encryption with SSE-KMS for every bucket in every account. Use a separate KMS key in each account.",
      "Turn on S3 Block Public Access for the buckets that hold customer data. Leave the other buckets on their current settings.",
      "Create an AWS Config rule that finds public buckets and notifies the security team of each one."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["The account-level setting overrides bucket policies and ACLs that would grant public access, for every bucket in the account.",
      "The SCP stops account administrators from turning the account-level setting off.",
      "Encryption at rest does not stop a bucket or object from being made public.",
      "This leaves other buckets open, and administrators could still change the setting.",
      "Config detects public buckets after they become public. It does not prevent it."] },

{ id: "d1-46", d: 1,
  q: "A company's web application lets customers download invoices that are stored as private objects in Amazon S3. Each download link must work only for the customer who requested it and must stop working after 15 minutes. The bucket must stay private. The application already runs with an IAM role that can read the invoice objects.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Have the application generate an S3 presigned URL that expires after 15 minutes to allow temporary access to the invoice.",
      "Have the application copy each requested invoice to a public S3 bucket and delete the copy after 15 minutes.",
      "Add an S3 bucket policy statement that allows s3:GetObject from the customer's IP address for 15 minutes.",
      "Make the invoice object public with an object ACL. Remove the ACL after 15 minutes with an AWS Lambda function."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A presigned URL grants temporary access to one object by using the application's credentials, and it expires at the set time. The bucket stays private.",
      "Anyone who finds the public copy could download it during those 15 minutes.",
      "Changing the bucket policy for every download is impractical, and IP addresses can be shared by many users.",
      "The object would be public for 15 minutes, which the company does not allow."] },

{ id: "d1-47", d: 1,
  q: "A company stores patient records in an Amazon DynamoDB table. Auditors require the company to control the key that encrypts the table, to be able to disable that key, and to see each use of the key in AWS CloudTrail. The table serves a production application and must stay online during the change.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Encrypt the table with a customer managed AWS KMS key to provide full control of the key.",
      "Keep the default encryption with an AWS owned KMS key and turn on CloudTrail data events for the table.",
      "Encrypt the table with the AWS managed KMS key for DynamoDB (aws/dynamodb) and turn on rotation for that key.",
      "Encrypt the items in the application with a key that is stored in a Lambda environment variable."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A customer managed key is created and controlled by the company, can be disabled, and each use of it is logged in CloudTrail.",
      "The company cannot see, manage, or disable an AWS owned key.",
      "The company cannot disable or change an AWS managed key, and AWS already rotates it.",
      "A key in an environment variable is not managed in AWS KMS, so it cannot be disabled centrally and its use is not logged."] },

{ id: "d1-48", d: 1,
  q: "Account A owns an Amazon S3 bucket whose objects are encrypted with SSE-KMS by using a customer managed key in account A. An IAM role in account B must read the objects, which must stay encrypted with that key. The role has an IAM policy that allows s3:GetObject on the bucket and kms:Decrypt on the key, but requests fail with Access Denied.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Add a statement to the bucket policy in account A that allows the account B role to call s3:GetObject.",
      "Add a statement to the key policy in account A that allows the account B role to call kms:Decrypt.",
      "Create a grant for the key from account B that allows the role to call kms:Decrypt.",
      "Copy the KMS key into account B and update the role's IAM policy to use the copied key.",
      "Turn off SSE-KMS on the bucket and copy the objects again with SSE-S3 encryption. Update the bucket policy for the account B role."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["For cross-account access, the bucket owner must also allow the role. The bucket policy statement grants that permission.",
      "The key owner must also allow the other account. Without a key policy statement, the IAM policy in account B is not enough to use the key.",
      "Account B cannot create grants on a key that it does not own unless account A has already allowed it.",
      "KMS keys cannot be copied between accounts, and the objects stay encrypted under the original key.",
      "The objects must stay encrypted with the customer managed key, so removing SSE-KMS breaks the requirement."] },

{ id: "d1-49", d: 1,
  q: "A company runs 40 AWS Lambda functions that read about 200 configuration values, such as feature flags and endpoint URLs, and one third-party API key. The values change a few times a month, and the API key does not need automatic rotation. The API key must be encrypted at rest. The functions read the values when they start.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements MOST cost-effectively?",
  o: ["Store the values as standard parameters in AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store, with the API key as a SecureString parameter.",
      "Store each configuration value and the API key as a separate secret in AWS Secrets Manager. Cache the values in each function.",
      "Store the values and the API key as advanced parameters in AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store.",
      "Store the values and the API key in one Amazon S3 object encrypted with SSE-KMS. Read it on each invocation."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Standard parameters have no storage charge, and a SecureString parameter encrypts the API key with AWS KMS. Rotation is not needed, so Secrets Manager adds nothing here.",
      "Secrets Manager charges for each secret each month, so 201 secrets cost far more than parameters.",
      "Advanced parameters have a monthly charge for each parameter, which standard parameters do not.",
      "Reading the object on every invocation adds S3 and KMS request charges that grow with traffic."] },

{ id: "d1-50", d: 1,
  q: "A company has 80 AWS accounts in AWS Organizations. Auditors require a record of API activity in every account and Region, delivered to one Amazon S3 bucket in a log archive account. Member account administrators must not be able to turn off this logging, and new accounts must be covered automatically.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Create an AWS CloudTrail organization trail for all Regions from the management account. Deliver the logs to the bucket in the log archive account.",
      "Create a multi-Region CloudTrail trail in each member account that delivers to the central bucket. Add the trail to each new account during onboarding.",
      "Turn on AWS Config in every account and Region. Send the configuration history to the central bucket through an aggregator.",
      "Create an Amazon EventBridge rule in each account that forwards all API events to a central event bus for storage."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An organization trail logs events for every account in the organization, including new accounts. Member accounts can see the trail but cannot change or delete it.",
      "Member administrators could stop their own trails, and every new account needs setup.",
      "AWS Config records resource configuration changes, not a full record of API activity.",
      "This needs rules in every account and custom storage, and administrators could change the rules."] },

{ id: "d1-51", d: 1,
  q: "A company must keep every Amazon S3 bucket in an account set to block public access. When a bucket becomes noncompliant, the setting must be fixed automatically within minutes, and the company needs a history of each bucket's compliance over time. The account has about 400 buckets, and teams create new buckets every week.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Use an AWS Config managed rule for S3 public access. Add an automatic remediation with a Systems Manager Automation runbook that blocks public access.",
      "Use an AWS Config managed rule for S3 public access settings. Send each noncompliant result to an Amazon SNS topic that alerts the security team.",
      "Run Amazon Macie jobs every hour to find public buckets. Have the security team block public access on each bucket that Macie reports.",
      "Create an Amazon EventBridge rule that matches PutBucketPolicy events. Invoke an AWS Lambda function that turns on Block Public Access for the bucket."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["AWS Config evaluates each bucket, keeps a compliance timeline, and runs the remediation runbook automatically when a bucket becomes noncompliant.",
      "Notifications alone do not fix the setting, so the bucket stays noncompliant until someone acts.",
      "Macie finds sensitive data. The fix here depends on people and could take much longer than minutes.",
      "This can fix some changes, but it keeps no compliance history and misses changes made through other API calls."] },

{ id: "d1-52", d: 1,
  q: "A payment application runs on Amazon EC2 instances that an Auto Scaling group manages, and an Application Load Balancer (ALB) receives client traffic. A compliance rule requires that traffic be encrypted from clients to the ALB and from the ALB to the instances. The company wants AWS to manage the public certificate that clients see.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Add an HTTPS listener to the ALB with a public certificate from AWS Certificate Manager (ACM).",
      "Use a target group with the HTTPS protocol. Install a certificate on each instance for the ALB connections.",
      "Add a TCP listener on port 443 to the ALB and pass the encrypted traffic through to the instances.",
      "Use a target group with the HTTP protocol on port 443 to reach the instances. Keep the HTTPS listener on the ALB.",
      "Set the target group protocol version to HTTP/2 and keep the HTTP protocol on port 80. Keep the HTTPS listener on the ALB for clients."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["The HTTPS listener ends the client's TLS connection with an ACM certificate that AWS renews.",
      "An HTTPS target group makes the ALB open a new TLS connection to each instance, which encrypts the second leg.",
      "Application Load Balancers support only HTTP and HTTPS listeners. TCP passthrough needs a Network Load Balancer.",
      "The port number does not encrypt anything. The HTTP protocol sends the traffic in plaintext.",
      "HTTP/2 over the HTTP protocol is still unencrypted between the ALB and the instances."] },

{ id: "d1-53", d: 1,
  q: "A company runs workloads on Amazon EC2 instances and stores container images in Amazon ECR. The security team needs continuous scanning for software vulnerabilities and unintended network exposure, with results updated automatically when new vulnerabilities are published. The team does not want to buy and run a separate scanning product.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Turn on Amazon Inspector for EC2 scanning and Amazon ECR enhanced scanning.",
      "Turn on Amazon GuardDuty Runtime Monitoring for the EC2 instances and the container workloads. Review the findings in the GuardDuty console.",
      "Turn on Amazon ECR basic scanning on push. Run a weekly AWS Systems Manager Patch Manager scan on the instances.",
      "Turn on AWS Security Hub with the AWS Foundational Security Best Practices standard for the account. Review the failed controls each week."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Amazon Inspector continuously scans EC2 instances and ECR images for software vulnerabilities and network reachability, and it rescans when new vulnerabilities are published.",
      "Runtime Monitoring detects threats in running workloads. It does not scan for software vulnerabilities.",
      "Basic scanning runs when an image is pushed, not continuously, and neither tool checks network exposure.",
      "This standard checks resource configurations against best practices. It does not scan software for vulnerabilities."] },

{ id: "d1-54", d: 1,
  q: "A company runs a MySQL database on Amazon EC2. The operations team spends many hours each month patching the operating system and the database engine and managing backups. The company wants AWS to take on these tasks while it keeps using MySQL. The database is about 500 GB and uses only standard MySQL features.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Run the database on Amazon RDS for MySQL instead. Set a maintenance window and an automated backup retention period.",
      "Keep the database on EC2 and turn on AWS Systems Manager Patch Manager for the operating system.",
      "Move the database to an EC2 Dedicated Host, where AWS takes over patching of the operating system and the database engine.",
      "Migrate the database to Amazon DynamoDB, which removes all patching and backup tasks."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["With RDS, AWS patches the operating system and the database engine during the maintenance window and takes automated backups, while the company keeps MySQL.",
      "Patch Manager automates OS patching, but the team still patches the database engine and manages backups.",
      "A Dedicated Host is physical capacity for the company's instances. The company still patches its own software on it.",
      "DynamoDB is not MySQL, so the application would need to be rewritten."] },

{ id: "d1-55", d: 1,
  q: "A company's workloads in private subnets reach the internet through NAT gateways. The security team must allow outbound HTTPS traffic only to an approved list of domain names, such as software update sites, and block all other outbound web traffic. The approved list changes a few times each month and must be updated in one central place.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Deploy AWS Network Firewall with a domain list rule group. Route the NAT gateway subnets' internet traffic through the firewall endpoints.",
      "Add outbound rules to the workloads' security groups that allow HTTPS only to each approved domain name and no other destination.",
      "Add outbound deny rules to the private subnets' network ACLs for the IP addresses of every website that is not on the approved list.",
      "Put an AWS WAF web ACL on the NAT gateways. Add a rule that allows outbound requests only to the approved domain names."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Network Firewall domain list rules inspect the server name in HTTPS traffic and allow or deny it by domain. Routing through the firewall endpoints puts all outbound traffic under the rules.",
      "Security group rules use IP addresses, prefix lists, or security groups. They cannot match domain names.",
      "The list of websites that are not approved is endless and keeps changing, so this cannot be maintained.",
      "AWS WAF protects resources such as load balancers and APIs from incoming requests. It cannot be associated with a NAT gateway."] },

{ id: "d1-56", d: 1,
  q: "A company has 50 accounts in AWS Organizations, and teams create new Application Load Balancers (ALBs) every week. The security team must make sure that a standard AWS WAF web ACL protects every ALB in every account, including new ALBs and new accounts. The security team wants to manage this from one central account.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements in the MOST operationally efficient way?",
  o: ["Use AWS Firewall Manager with an AWS WAF policy that applies the web ACL to all ALBs in the organization.",
      "Deploy the web ACL to each account with AWS CloudFormation StackSets. Have each team associate it with every new ALB that it creates.",
      "Create an AWS Config rule that finds ALBs without the web ACL. Notify the team that owns each ALB that the rule finds.",
      "Create one web ACL in the security account and share it with every account through AWS Resource Access Manager (AWS RAM)."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Firewall Manager applies the web ACL across accounts and automatically adds new ALBs and new accounts that are in the policy scope.",
      "This depends on each team remembering to associate the web ACL with each new ALB.",
      "This finds gaps after they appear and still relies on teams to fix them.",
      "Web ACLs cannot be shared through AWS RAM, so the other accounts could not use it."] },

{ id: "d1-57", d: 1,
  q: "A company runs three microservices as Amazon ECS tasks on AWS Fargate. One service writes to an Amazon SQS queue, one reads from an Amazon DynamoDB table, and one reads from an Amazon S3 bucket. Each service must have only the permissions that it needs. Each service has its own task definition, and none of the services may use long-term credentials.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create a separate IAM role for each service with only its permissions. Set each role as the task role in that service's task definition.",
      "Create one IAM role with the permissions of all three services. Set the role as the task role in all three task definitions.",
      "Create a separate IAM role for each service with only its permissions. Set each role as the task execution role in that service's task definition.",
      "Create an IAM user for each service with only its permissions. Pass the user's access keys to the containers as environment variables."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A task role gives the containers in a task temporary credentials for that role. A role for each service keeps each one to least privilege.",
      "Every service would get the permissions of all three, which breaks least privilege.",
      "The task execution role is used by ECS to pull images and send logs. The application code does not get its permissions.",
      "Access keys are long-term credentials that can leak from environment variables, and roles make them unnecessary."] },

{ id: "d1-58", d: 1,
  q: "A company runs an Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL DB instance in us-east-1. A disaster recovery policy requires point-in-time restore of the database in us-west-2 if us-east-1 becomes unavailable. The company does not need a running database in us-west-2. The database holds 2 TB of data that changes constantly during business hours.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Turn on cross-Region automated backups replication to us-west-2 for the DB instance.",
      "Create a cross-Region read replica in us-west-2. Promote the read replica if us-east-1 becomes unavailable.",
      "Take a manual snapshot each day and copy it to us-west-2 with an AWS Lambda function to provide a recovery point.",
      "Convert the DB instance to Multi-AZ, with a standby in another Availability Zone."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Replicated automated backups copy snapshots and transaction logs to the other Region, which supports point-in-time restore there without a running database.",
      "A read replica is a running database, which the company does not need, and promotion does not restore to a chosen point in time.",
      "Daily snapshots restore only to the time of each snapshot, not to any point in time.",
      "The standby is in the same Region, so it does not help if us-east-1 becomes unavailable."] },

{ id: "d1-59", d: 1,
  q: "A company is designing a VPC for a three-tier web application in two Availability Zones. Only the load balancer may be reachable from the internet. The application servers must reach the internet for updates, and the database must accept connections only from the application servers. Each tier must run in both Availability Zones.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select THREE.)",
  o: ["Place an internet-facing Application Load Balancer in public subnets in both Availability Zones.",
      "Place the application servers in private subnets. Route their internet traffic through NAT gateways in the public subnets.",
      "Place the database in private subnets with a security group that allows the database port only from the application servers' security group.",
      "Place the application servers in public subnets with public IP addresses and security groups that allow traffic only from the load balancer.",
      "Place the database in public subnets with a network ACL that allows the database port from the whole VPC CIDR range."],
  a: [0, 1, 2],
  r: ["The load balancer is the only internet-facing component and spreads traffic across both zones.",
      "Private subnets keep the servers unreachable from the internet, and NAT gateways let them start outbound connections for updates.",
      "The security group reference allows only the application servers to reach the database, which has no internet route.",
      "Servers with public IP addresses in public subnets are reachable from the internet if a rule is ever loosened, which breaks the design.",
      "A database in a public subnet is exposed, and the network ACL would allow every resource in the VPC."] },

{ id: "d1-60", d: 1,
  q: "A company plans to store regulated files on a new Amazon EFS file system that Linux instances will mount. The data must be encrypted at rest with a customer managed AWS KMS key and encrypted in transit between the instances and the file system. Several hundred instances in three Availability Zones will mount the file system.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Create the EFS file system with encryption at rest turned on, and choose the customer managed key.",
      "Mount the file system with the Amazon EFS mount helper and the tls mount option on each instance.",
      "Create the EFS file system without encryption. Turn on encryption at rest later from the console.",
      "Mount the EFS file system with the standard NFS client and turn on IPsec on each instance's network adapter.",
      "Turn on S3 Bucket Keys for the EFS file system to reduce AWS KMS request costs."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Encryption at rest is chosen when an EFS file system is created, and it can use a customer managed key.",
      "The mount helper with the tls option encrypts NFS traffic between the instance and the file system.",
      "Encryption at rest cannot be turned on for an existing unencrypted file system.",
      "This is not how EFS encrypts traffic in transit. The mount helper handles TLS for each mount.",
      "S3 Bucket Keys are an Amazon S3 feature and do not apply to Amazon EFS."] },
]);
